-- Additive sync from the frozen v1 roster (60 players, live at time of
-- first deployment) up to the current 80-player roster, generated by
-- comparing scripts/export-ranked-roster.mjs's full computed state against
-- a live read of ranked_private.{candidates,players,memberships,rivals} via
-- `supabase db query --linked` (see session transcript for the exact diff).
--
-- Deliberately additive-only, per this project's stated policy for the
-- frozen ruleset (see 202609130002_ranked_roster.sql's own header comment):
-- inserts only rows for the 20 players added since the DB was last synced
-- (all 5 tables' existing rows for the original 60 were diffed and found
-- byte-identical - zero label/country/position/membership drift - so
-- nothing existing is touched, and no existing player's rivals pool is
-- expanded to include the 20 new names: that would be a ruleset-version
-- change, not a data sync, and is out of scope here).
--
-- Verified before writing: 13 existing ranked_private.results rows (2
-- accounts) all reference player_id values in the original 60, none of
-- which are touched by this migration.
--
-- Every insert carries "on conflict do nothing": the live database was 60
-- players when this was written and applied cleanly, but
-- 202609130002_ranked_roster.sql's own content has since been regenerated
-- in place across later roster-expansion batches (a pattern that
-- contradicts its own "do not overwrite after production play" header -
-- worth not repeating) and now already contains the same 80 players this
-- file adds. A from-scratch replay (a fresh deploy, or this project's local
-- test harness, which always starts from an empty database) therefore
-- already has these 20 players from 202609130002 alone; on conflict do
-- nothing makes this file a safe no-op in that case instead of a duplicate-
-- key error, while still being exactly what actually ran against the real,
-- already-60-player production database.
